WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:06.089
So hello everybody, and definitely excited to be here again.

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:10.679
I think the last time I was here outside of Jennifer's class

00:00:10.699 --> 00:00:17.079
was the Entrepreneur Week and VAS. I came in to do something

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:20.579
with VAS. But anyway, it was very exciting, a lot of

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:26.689
exchanges. And I am focused today on personal branding and

00:00:26.709 --> 00:00:30.929
of course utilizing social media. One thing I just did was I

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:36.789
twitted out this as Stanford base. So for everybody who is

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:42.839
on Twitter, right click, just kind of check on my Twitter if

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:45.609
you right click, and the notes are there, the random notes,

00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:48.609
just random. It says Stanford base. And if you click on it,

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:52.359
it has like four or five or six or seven or eight or nine or

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:55.609
10 or 12 little thoughts, and I will be working kind of from

00:00:55.609 --> 00:01:01.319
those thoughts.

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:02.339
[Informal Talk]

00:01:02.339 --> 00:01:04.840
Because there is a real correlation between personal

00:01:04.859 --> 00:01:07.700
branding and corporate branding, and I have been watching

00:01:07.719 --> 00:01:10.469
all things that are happening right now with some of my

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:15.510
favorite companies out there. Obviously, I am a big fan of

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.239
all the social media companies. Tristan, I met right here.

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:22.750
Tristan came out of - I actually met him first at an event.

00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:26.049
He is over at Foursquare now but I think we can talk to

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:30.500
Tristan all the time. So Foursquare is one of the companies

00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:35.540
I really think that has got it right along with, obviously,

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.939
traditional companies and ecosystem around Twitter and some

00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:44.000
others.

00:01:44.019 --> 00:01:45.019
[Informal Talk]

00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:46.019
So

00:01:46.019 --> 00:01:48.159
[inaudible] I will jump right into it. I just came in from

00:01:48.179 --> 00:01:52.620
Atlanta. I was at the Black Enterprise Entrepreneurs

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:59.170
Conference 2011. I had a good time with that and got back in

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:03.409
probably around midnight or 1 o'clock. I am doing like you

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:05.679
guys are doing final week, running both hands. I

00:02:05.679 --> 00:02:09.789
[inaudible] some random questions about some current events,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.349
nobody knew anything. It's like everybody is unplugged from

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:22.089
everything. So how many friends of Lady Gaga? See, now we

00:02:22.109 --> 00:02:26.099
go. Yes, a bunch of Lady Gaga fans.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.559
So one thing I wanted to emphasize was personal branding. I

00:02:29.579 --> 00:02:34.989
want to define it, and defining a personal brand, Lady Gaga

00:02:35.009 --> 00:02:39.719
is a very good example. I would argue with anyone that the

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:43.699
culture of her brand, and by the way personal brand equates

00:02:43.719 --> 00:02:47.329
to the culture of view so the culture of you. So the culture

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:54.079
of view utilizing Lady Gaga, her culture is now bigger than

00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:57.719
the music and the music is good and it's very big. And what

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:00.829
do I mean by that and how did she end up defining that

00:03:00.829 --> 00:03:05.959
culture? Well, from one aspect, early on, the community

00:03:05.979 --> 00:03:09.979
tried to define what a brand was. And the thing about that

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.579
is that the community may not even be fans of your product.

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:18.169
So we have the behaviors at iTunes, there is a group of

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:22.569
individuals at iTunes. No matter whose album comes out, it

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:27.759
doesn't matter. They go to that artist site and they attack,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.829
one star, one star, half a star, I hate it, I hate it, I

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:33.829
hate it. But they don't even like the band. They are not

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:35.329
even a fan of the band.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:38.619
So it sends out the wrong message to non-fans because they

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:41.469
really think that someone is reviewing the product, the

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:46.059
album and giving it negative reviews. So one of the great

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:49.019
things about social media is what, we get to control the

00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:51.859
messaging to a certain extent. So one of things you want to

00:03:51.859 --> 00:03:54.859
always do is have your fans go in your night when your

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:57.969
product is being released and give it the five stars all the

00:03:57.989 --> 00:04:01.859
way down. But there was a group trying to define her brand

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:04.719
early on, who is Lady Gaga, what she is about, blah, blah,

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:09.059
blah. Finally, her in-house camp took control of the

00:04:09.079 --> 00:04:12.649
message. So once they took control of the message, you

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:18.480
started to see who she is, socially, politically and to find

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.530
her look. Her look is progressive, by the way, so what it is

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.120
today is not the same as two years ago. But what it does is

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:32.030
it separates her immediately from all of the other 20

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:35.060
million bands that are out there that are trying to carve

00:04:35.079 --> 00:04:39.129
their niche. So he was like a huge fan of Lady Gaga, like a

00:04:39.149 --> 00:04:43.560
huge fan, a big fan.

00:04:43.579 --> 00:04:46.729
So what would you say to culture of her brand is if you were

00:04:46.729 --> 00:04:47.729
to summarize it?

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:48.729
[Audience]

00:04:48.729 --> 00:05:18.729
So that's a lot of information. That's a lot of information.

00:05:26.269 --> 00:05:29.790
So if you are pointing out about seven or eight things that

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:33.870
defined her brand, #1, she mobilized her audience and gave

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:38.959
them a name. They are the monsters. So this means that in

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:44.459
many respects, my fans are my fans. So when I speak, I am

00:05:44.479 --> 00:05:47.479
not speaking to you, I am not speaking even to the people

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:51.639
who go on iTunes or Amazon or anywhere else who are not fans

00:05:51.659 --> 00:05:55.569
and make negative comments because I make music for the

00:05:55.589 --> 00:05:59.649
monsters. That's very important. That's also very important

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:03.170
for corporations out there who - this is why the analytics

00:06:03.189 --> 00:06:06.290
work so well. With the analytics, you are able to look at

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.610
the information, then do your targeting and say this is who

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:13.250
I am trying to reach, this is what it's all about. We are

00:06:13.269 --> 00:06:17.959
doing music right now in a way it's never been done before

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:20.939
because of all the social media tools that are available.

00:06:20.959 --> 00:06:25.540
Another thing that you pointed out about Lady Gaga is that

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.060
she has a social and a political message. And I think it was

00:06:30.079 --> 00:06:33.079
recently - I am not sure - but I believe it was target of

00:06:33.079 --> 00:06:37.160
one of the retailers that had a difference of opinion, I

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:42.360
believe, with her message or the fans of her music or a

00:06:42.379 --> 00:06:45.910
cause that she didn't agree with. So she made it known to

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:50.180
them, "Listen, if you are not going to support my monsters

00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:56.550
and their ideology, then I don't necessarily want you to

00:06:56.569 --> 00:06:59.740
carry my product." Now, I don't know which store it was for

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.750
sure since we are live streaming. But her political and

00:07:02.769 --> 00:07:06.160
social position is much part of the culture of a brand as a

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.180
music.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.300
So if you take personal branding today, and you want to

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:14.370
separate yourself, if you want to do it the most efficient

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:20.430
way, start defining your brand early. Define who you are.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:23.449
it's no longer good enough to be the best singer even though

00:07:23.449 --> 00:07:24.449
there is a new

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:27.689
[inaudible] voice and it's about focusing on the voice.

00:07:27.709 --> 00:07:30.670
Previously, you could just focus on the voice and the best

00:07:30.689 --> 00:07:34.399
singer would be the one. But today, with transparency, real

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:38.709
time all this information available 24 hours a day, you have

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:42.829
to be willing to define what your band is, what the culture

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:47.350
of your brand is early on, and it will help you to carve out

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:50.910
your niche and kind of take off and get out in front of

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.410
everybody, or at least theoretically, it allowed you to take

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:10.089
off. So I am going to align --

00:08:10.109 --> 00:08:11.109
[Informal Talk]

00:08:11.109 --> 00:08:13.359
So I was going to give an example of Cisco. How many are

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:18.399
familiar with product from Cisco? So what's one of the

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:20.419
products from Cisco that you are familiar with?

00:08:20.419 --> 00:08:21.419
[Audience]

00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:28.089
Routers, anybody else?

00:08:28.109 --> 00:08:29.109
[Audience]

00:08:29.109 --> 00:08:32.789
Switches, and yours was most important one because this is

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:36.059
the point that I am going to make. So Cisco was known for

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:40.600
switches and routers. Now, this is just analogy between

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:43.870
personal branding, corporate branding showing that basically

00:08:43.889 --> 00:08:48.110
there is a lot of parallels. So inside it now that the new

00:08:48.129 --> 00:08:51.079
step will be and this is going back about 18 months, they

00:08:51.079 --> 00:08:56.100
are going to get into their own devices. They want to brand

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.279
hardware. They want to put Cisco on things, and they took is

00:09:00.299 --> 00:09:03.370
great piece of product that I am a big fan of called the

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:10.840
Flip. And the problem from my perspective is that they didn'

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.189
t utilize what I am calling again "marathon branding" and

00:09:15.209 --> 00:09:20.049
start to seed the public with the idea that Cisco would now

00:09:20.069 --> 00:09:24.409
offer more than just routers and switches.

00:09:24.429 --> 00:09:28.090
It's hard to get people to adjust to a new behavior from

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:33.019
nowhere. As I go down 10 miles and make a ride, that's where

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:36.850
the milk and the flowery is, and so that becomes

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:41.090
subconsciously my habit. If you move the milk somewhere else

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:44.439
for the first couple of months, I still want to get there,

00:09:44.459 --> 00:09:47.459
start to make that right and then adjust and make the left.

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:50.980
No one was looking for hardware from Cisco. They weren't

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.089
looking for earphones, they are not looking for anything,

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:55.529
they are looking for switches, routers, infrastructure. So

00:09:55.549 --> 00:09:59.370
the marathon branding approach is the idea - beyond the

00:09:59.389 --> 00:10:02.799
lining thing, it's the culture of you - but the idea that

00:10:02.819 --> 00:10:08.730
you start to see the new behavior early and you stick with

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.379
it. So I think one of the challenges that Cisco had was

00:10:12.399 --> 00:10:17.970
that, #1, they weren't willing to run the marathon. They

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:23.059
gave it a shot. They tried, and much to my dismay, because I

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:26.329
am a big fan of the Flip, they decided to kill the product.

00:10:26.329 --> 00:10:29.129
It's no longer even in the product line anymore, and it went

00:10:29.129 --> 00:10:30.129
away.

00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:35.279
So when you are establishing a new behavior, when you are a

00:10:35.299 --> 00:10:38.049
brand and it's been around, and you want to transition, you

00:10:38.049 --> 00:10:41.309
want to start to introduce new features, new products, you

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:44.309
have to see to behavior early. Another example I wanted to

00:10:44.329 --> 00:10:50.210
give is Google. As Google was beginning to move into music,

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:55.850
which is not what you think, you Google for information, you

00:10:55.870 --> 00:11:02.389
Google for Gmail, check your Gmail. You don't necessarily

00:11:02.409 --> 00:11:08.149
Google for music, not yet. Now, there is rumors and there is

00:11:08.169 --> 00:11:12.559
links and things that say that now Google is going to focus

00:11:12.579 --> 00:11:16.990
on music. How many people in are aware of that? So there is

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:20.819
10 or 12 people who are aware that music is going to be a

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:22.549
part of the culture of Google.

00:11:22.569 --> 00:11:27.029
Well, my suggestion would be to Google, take a page out of

00:11:27.049 --> 00:11:30.049
the music industry one of the very few pages that you would

00:11:30.049 --> 00:11:33.059
take because it took a long time and the music industry is

00:11:33.079 --> 00:11:36.960
still adjusting to what this new world is, but take a page

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.019
out just from this

00:11:38.039 --> 00:11:41.870
[inaudible], link it. When you hear the music is being

00:11:41.889 --> 00:11:47.220
linked from an artist, it's normally the labels themselves

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:50.590
linking it. Nobody hacked into a server, none of that

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:54.379
happened. This is a way of beta testing the product, hoping

00:11:54.399 --> 00:11:58.389
that the community will decide what the single is. If Google

00:11:58.409 --> 00:12:01.429
is going to be a place for music, then they should go ahead

00:12:01.429 --> 00:12:04.699
and step out now and start to talk about Google being the

00:12:04.719 --> 00:12:06.319
place for music.

00:12:06.339 --> 00:12:11.199
And accompanying that did it and does it very well,

00:12:11.219 --> 00:12:15.720
Microsoft, they are in the marathon branding. They will take

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:21.360
something like the Xbox which is a great gaming device,

00:12:21.379 --> 00:12:26.080
great console. And they will say, "Listen, we are late to

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:29.350
the space, we didn't get in early. We are going to set aside

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:32.350
x amount of dollars, and we want to carve. Well, we didn't

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:36.750
lose money on the hardware. Xbox is not a profitable piece

00:12:36.769 --> 00:12:40.350
of hardware but we are in for the long haul. We will fine-

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:43.189
tune the product but we are here," and they started talking

00:12:43.209 --> 00:12:46.799
about it, they delivered. It's a great console, center of

00:12:46.819 --> 00:12:50.240
your living room, and they rolled it out. Today, all of us -

00:12:50.259 --> 00:12:53.509
not all of us - but at some point, if you like games, you

00:12:53.509 --> 00:12:57.409
have had an Xbox. And they integrated, which again I should

00:12:57.429 --> 00:13:00.179
actually say I participated in that. I went down to New

00:13:00.179 --> 00:13:04.179
Zealand and Australia and helped introduce the social medial

00:13:04.199 --> 00:13:09.470
integration into the Xbox a couple of years ago. But I only

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:13.539
put that example so that you could see that Xbox Microsoft

00:13:13.559 --> 00:13:18.590
even with Bing search, everybody knows what Bing is now.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:24.789
So Microsoft has a history of going the distance in terms of

00:13:24.809 --> 00:13:28.169
branding, marathon branding, getting in, sticking with it

00:13:28.189 --> 00:13:32.019
and riding it. On this one point with Cisco - because I don'

00:13:32.039 --> 00:13:35.039
t know what the other things they are doing - but when they

00:13:35.039 --> 00:13:40.610
decided to go from infrastructure switches to hardware, I

00:13:40.629 --> 00:13:42.949
don't think that they thought that they would stick it out.

00:13:42.949 --> 00:13:46.199
It was a test; it was an open test without declaring it as a

00:13:46.199 --> 00:13:49.949
test. So the

00:13:49.969 --> 00:13:52.379
[inaudible] between personal branding and corporate branding

00:13:52.399 --> 00:13:56.230
are very similar, and they will be even more when you take a

00:13:56.250 --> 00:14:01.829
look - now, Apple, one of my favorites, all time, how many

00:14:01.849 --> 00:14:06.590
Apple fans in here? The whole room.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:11.539
Apple has a way of making sure that you know what they are

00:14:11.559 --> 00:14:15.120
doing without saying they are going to do it. They do it

00:14:15.139 --> 00:14:19.709
very well. They make sure you know what's coming without

00:14:19.729 --> 00:14:24.970
saying what's coming but it is coming. And right now, the

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:28.250
next thing that they are going to do - and I will come back

00:14:28.269 --> 00:14:31.519
to this - but the next thing they are going to do is they

00:14:31.519 --> 00:14:34.269
are going to offer the locker. They are going to beat

00:14:34.269 --> 00:14:36.329
everybody to music storage, which the competitors in that

00:14:36.329 --> 00:14:45.220
space, Amazon, the music industry itself, well in some

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:48.769
aspects, possibly Facebook and Google, I don't know the

00:14:48.789 --> 00:14:52.279
intimate details, I am just throwing it out there. But here

00:14:52.299 --> 00:14:55.649
comes Apple again out of nowhere, how do they do it and how

00:14:55.669 --> 00:14:59.629
are they getting it done and how do they do that so fast.

00:14:59.649 --> 00:15:03.169
Well, one is that once they make a decision, it seems like

00:15:03.189 --> 00:15:07.529
for the most part, they stick with it. They link up, and

00:15:07.549 --> 00:15:10.029
they make it known without making it known that "We are

00:15:10.029 --> 00:15:13.129
coming, you better be ready and when we do it, we will make

00:15:13.129 --> 00:15:15.629
it user friendly, the interface would be great, the personal

00:15:15.629 --> 00:15:18.129
experience would be great and the design would be great."

00:15:18.139 --> 00:15:23.179
So the personal branding and corporate branding, they are

00:15:23.199 --> 00:15:26.870
very much aligned whether it's an artist or whether it's a

00:15:26.889 --> 00:15:31.919
product. What I was going to say about Apple in the music

00:15:31.939 --> 00:15:37.490
industry was a few years ago, there was these deals, these

00:15:37.509 --> 00:15:41.649
licensing deals that were being cut that helped to set up

00:15:41.669 --> 00:15:44.860
the iTunes Store. So all labels had to participate. And I

00:15:44.879 --> 00:15:47.539
was happened to be with some of my music industry friends

00:15:47.539 --> 00:15:53.819
that the music industry is no longer in Hollywood or

00:15:53.839 --> 00:15:56.649
anywhere else in New York but it's really based in Silicon

00:15:56.649 --> 00:15:59.559
Valley. And of course, at that time, they laughed, "The

00:15:59.579 --> 00:16:03.980
music industry is not in Silicon Valley." I said, "Yes, it

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.759
is, it's now here." It started with the creation of the mp3.

00:16:08.779 --> 00:16:13.250
Once the community had the ability to download music and

00:16:13.269 --> 00:16:19.149
share music, the control of the music industry left

00:16:19.169 --> 00:16:23.600
Hollywood, New York, and now it was controlled globally by

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:26.409
the community. But of course, they couldn't understand it at

00:16:26.429 --> 00:16:31.659
the time. Well, the next piece was when Apple was able to

00:16:31.679 --> 00:16:35.250
make a deal with the labels for this little project, just

00:16:35.269 --> 00:16:38.279
another little project we are doing, called the iTunes

00:16:38.299 --> 00:16:41.949
Store, and you will be able to buy music here. Well, now,

00:16:41.969 --> 00:16:45.819
it's no longer speculation but the bulk of all music is

00:16:45.839 --> 00:16:51.039
bought through Apple, and clearly the head of the music

00:16:51.059 --> 00:16:55.039
industry, the power, the control is here in Silicon Valley.

00:16:55.059 --> 00:16:59.679
So when you are defining a brand, and again what was Apple

00:16:59.699 --> 00:17:03.709
at that time, by the way? Before Apple was music, what was

00:17:03.729 --> 00:17:06.450
Apple by the way? What did you see Apple as, personal

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:10.660
computer? I mean no, not personal computers, but it was

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:14.829
about video. At the end of the day, it was about art, people

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:18.759
who are into art. They loved Apple products. Later on, after

00:17:18.779 --> 00:17:26.019
they took over the music business and now - Netflix, they

